      "term": "Ubi Dolorianism",
      "caseSensitive": true,
      "short": "A homespun, flowery folk version of Dolorian architecture, brought to Insulinde by Ubi herdsmen who came on the first boats.",
      "details": "Conceptualization pictures an old wooden building on the coast as it once was (white and gold with light red flower motifs) and names the style, and you can ask whether you just made the term up. The young man Noid confirms it: 'folk Dolorianism', an early, mass-produced subset of Dolorian building. In his words, nearly everything between an ancient concrete cathedral and a glass cube counts as Dolorianism.",
      "spoiler": "Noid believes the Ubis built the old church about 320 years ago as a kind of sarcophagus: a structure meant to surround and contain something strange they had heard about but did not really understand.",
